    model:"The table compares the ways in which people in one city travelled to work in 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the car remained the most common way of getting to work in both years, although its share fell. The most significant change was the rise of electric scooters, which were not used at all in 2000.\n\nIn 2000, 40 percent of commuters travelled by car and 35 percent used a motorbike, so these two vehicles accounted for three quarters of all journeys. By 2020, both had become less popular, falling to 34 percent and 25 percent respectively. The percentage of people who cycled also dropped slightly, from 8 to 6 percent.\n\nIn contrast, the other three types of transport became more popular. Electric scooters grew from zero to 12 percent, which made them more popular than walking or cycling. The figure for buses rose from 15 to 18 percent, and the proportion of people who walked to work more than doubled, from 2 to 5 percent."
