    plan:["Mở bài: paraphrase hai biểu đồ","Overview: mối đe doạ lớn nhất + xu hướng chung của ba loài","Thân 1: biểu đồ tròn (nhóm các mối đe doạ do con người)","Thân 2: biểu đồ cột (hai loài giảm, một loài tăng)"],
    model:"The pie chart shows the main dangers facing wild animals in a particular region, and the bar chart compares the populations of three endangered species in 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, habitat loss is by far the biggest threat to wildlife. While the numbers of elephants and tigers fell over the twenty-year period, the sea turtle population grew.\n\nAccording to the pie chart, almost half of the threats (45%) come from habitat loss. Hunting is the second most serious problem, at 25 percent, followed by pollution at 15 percent. Climate change accounts for 10 percent, and other factors make up the remaining 5 percent. Together, the threats caused directly by human activity account for the large majority of the total.\n\nTurning to the bar chart, the number of elephants dropped from 300 to 180, and the tiger population more than halved, falling from 90 to just 40. In contrast, sea turtles increased from 500 to 650, which made them the only species to grow in number."
